WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.953
A signature is over bytes, so the first design question is exactly what
data is being signed.

2
00:00:04.953 --> 00:00:13.057
Human-readable content, JSON-like structures, files, request bodies,
headers, timestamps, and metadata can all be represented in multiple ways.

3
00:00:13.057 --> 00:00:18.267
If the signer and verifier do not agree on the exact byte representation,
verification may fail.

4
00:00:18.267 --> 00:00:22.576
Worse, the application may verify one representation while acting on
another.

5
00:00:22.576 --> 00:00:28.301
Canonicalization is the process of making data representation consistent
before signing or verifying.

6
00:00:28.301 --> 00:00:36.276
It can involve field ordering, whitespace, character encoding,
normalization, timestamp format, metadata selection, and message
boundaries.

7
00:00:36.276 --> 00:00:42.965
Structured data is especially tricky because two documents may appear
equivalent to a person but produce different bytes.

8
00:00:42.965 --> 00:00:47.275
The signing workflow should remove ambiguity before the signature is
created.

9
00:00:47.275 --> 00:00:48.819
Message boundaries matter.

10
00:00:48.819 --> 00:00:56.730
A system should know whether it signs only the body, selected headers, a
timestamp, a request path, a method, a file digest, or a complete
envelope.

11
00:00:56.730 --> 00:01:00.589
Detached signatures keep the signature separate from the signed data.

12
00:01:00.589 --> 00:01:04.641
Attached signature concepts bundle signature material with the content.

13
00:01:04.641 --> 00:01:11.266
Both can be valid patterns, but the application must understand what is
protected and what remains outside the signature.

14
00:01:11.266 --> 00:01:14.675
Signing the wrong representation can create trust confusion.

15
00:01:14.675 --> 00:01:19.435
If a webhook signs only part of a request, unsigned fields may still
influence behavior.

16
00:01:19.435 --> 00:01:26.188
If a token signs claims but the application trusts external parameters
too, the signature may not protect the full decision.

17
00:01:26.188 --> 00:01:35.000
A good signing workflow documents the protected data, the unprotected
context, and the business checks that happen after cryptographic
verification succeeds.
